    "body": "Relay BB run pushed an update.\n\nPull request: #351\n\nTokens: 0 · Cost: $0.0000\n\nPushed branch `bb/relay-233-auth-v1-06-08-bootstrap-the-first-acco-thr_g77y45yxgh` to origin.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplements issue #233: a `/setup` API flow that bootstraps the deployment's first (and only) account.\n\n- **`createAccount`** (`backend/packages/data/src/auth/authActions.ts`): the single email/password-validating, Argon2id-hashing account-creation function, shared by setup today and available to a future signup route without exposing one now.\n- **`bootstrapFirstAccount`**: available only while `users` is empty and the caller's token matches `SETUP_TOKEN`. A cheap unlocked pre-check short-circuits the common post-bootstrap case; the actual decision runs inside a transaction holding a Postgres advisory lock, so concurrent attempts produce exactly one winner. Returns 404 both when a user already exists (checked before the token) and for a wrong token, so the route never confirms whether setup is still open.\n- **`anyUserExists`** (`usersStore.ts`): backs the emptiness check.\n- **`setupHandler.ts`**: `POST /api/setup?token=...`, unauthenticated by design, wired into `serve.ts` alongside a new required `SETUP_TOKEN` env var (same convention as `SEMPREC_API_TOKEN`; provisioning the production secret file is out of scope per the issue).\n- Tests at both the action layer (including a concurrency race test) and the HTTP handler layer.\n\nVerified: unit tests (114/114), integration tests (721/721), TypeScript typecheck, ESLint, Prettier, and dependency-boundary checks all pass.\n\nRelay-Step-Status: pass",
